<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#vilayet">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Vilayet</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1864–1922</span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A vilayet was a first-level province of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> under the administrative system introduced by the Provincial Law of 1864, which replaced the older eyalets. Each was headed by a governor (vali) and divided into sanjaks and smaller districts. The system was applied across the empire from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a> and lasted until the end of the empire.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/vilayet.jpg" alt="Vilayet" width="320" height="468" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Grégoire Aristarchi / Gregory Aristarchis (Bey) / Γρηγόριος Αριστάρχης · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:L%C3%A9gislation_ottomane_ou_Recueil_des_Aristarchi-Bey_Gr%C3%A9goire_Tome2.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
